UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Benefic class and actual condition',body='Al-Bīrūnī (§§386–401) identifies Jupiter as moderately warm and moist, diurnal and the greater benefic. Benefic classification describes a traditional tendency; whether Jupiter can accomplish what it signifies depends on its essential and accidental condition. Its sect affiliation should not be mistaken for a guarantee of favorable judgment.',editorial_notes='Batch 07: precise source-dependent clarification or removal of duplicate interpretive language.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='jupiter') AND section_key='sect';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='The condition of a cutting and forceful planet',body='Mars''s traditional hot, dry character may signify initiative and disciplined force or, in other circumstances, excessive conflict and severing. Sect, essential dignity, angularity and the configurations involved qualify the judgment. These are circumstances affecting one planet, not evidence for distinct constructive and destructive planetary natures.',editorial_notes='Batch 07: precise source-dependent clarification or removal of duplicate interpretive language.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='mars') AND section_key='condition-layers';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Mercury''s conditional classification in al-Bīrūnī',body='In §§386–401 al-Bīrūnī calls Mercury moderately cold and dry, male and diurnal by nature, but says that it takes on the characteristics of nearby planets. This is a source-specific account of its changing expression; it should not be flattened into the statement that Mercury has no natural classification or that every author judges its sect identically.',editorial_notes='Batch 07: precise source-dependent clarification or removal of duplicate interpretive language.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='mercury') AND section_key='variability';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Natural beneficence and the nocturnal sect',body='Al-Bīrūnī (§§386–401) describes Venus as the lesser benefic, feminine and nocturnal, with a moderately cold and especially moist nature. These are its general classifications in his account; essential and accidental conditions still determine how its significations operate in a given chart.',editorial_notes='Batch 07: precise source-dependent clarification or removal of duplicate interpretive language.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='venus') AND section_key='sect';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Historical constitution, not a modern diagnosis',body='The four temperaments were categories within premodern natural philosophy and medicine. They should not be treated as four rigid modern personality types, nor should an astrological constitutional judgment be interpreted as clinical evidence. The historically attributed qualities and bodily humours belong to the source''s own explanatory framework.',editorial_notes='Batch 07: precise source-dependent clarification or removal of duplicate interpretive language.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='temperament') AND section_key='common-mistakes';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='A sign can belong to several classifications',body='A zodiacal sign is simultaneously classified by triplicity, seasonal modality, planetary rulership, gender and other traditional taxonomies. These are distinct relations, not alternative names for one quality. Al-Bīrūnī (§347) connects hot/cold and moist/dry sign classifications to the elements and humours, while reporting differences in Indian assignments. Those differences must remain attributed to their sources.',editorial_notes='Batch 07: precise source-dependent clarification or removal of duplicate interpretive language.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='zodiac-sign') AND section_key='multiple-classes';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='A reversal measured against the zodiac',body='Retrogradation is observed as decreasing ecliptic longitude for a planet that ordinarily advances eastward. It does not require the planet to reverse its physical orbit. The apparent effect results from relative motions and viewing geometry; first or daily motion, which carries the sky westward, is a separate reference motion.',editorial_notes='Batch 07: precise source-dependent clarification or removal of duplicate interpretive language.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='retrogradation') AND section_key='astronomical-basis';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Historical seasonal names and later terminology',body='Movable and tropical describe the signs beginning at equinoxes and solstices: Aries, Cancer, Libra and Capricorn. Cardinal is a later English name for the same group. Here tropical classifies a sign''s place in a seasonal quarter; it does not distinguish a tropical from a sidereal zodiac.',editorial_notes='Batch 07: precise source-dependent clarification or removal of duplicate interpretive language.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='tropical-sign') AND section_key='name';
